From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: patch <patch@local>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 02:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Decode progressive JPEGs whose DC scan is non-interleaved

A progressive JPEG may split its DC coefficients into one scan per component
(SOS with a single component, Ss=0 Se=0) instead of a single interleaved scan.
MozJPEG and most web optimizers emit that layout, so it is common in ripped
and scanned material.

The MCU loop in DecodeJPEG reads a Y block -- four of them when subsampled --
and then the chroma blocks of the same MCU. In a non-interleaved scan those
chroma bits are not there, so the reads consume bits belonging to the
following Y blocks and the bitstream desynchronizes. On a large image that
surfaces as a Huffman failure reported as a bare JPEG_DECODE_ERROR; on a
small one it does not fail at all and the preview is silently scrambled,
which is the worse half. Measured against a Pillow reference before this
patch: mean |diff| 107.6 (4:4:4) and 113.5 (4:2:0) on a 96x64 fixture that
reported success.

In a non-interleaved scan the data units run in raster order over the
component's own block grid -- ceil(width/8) x ceil(height/8) for luma,
whatever the subsampling -- and one data unit counts as one MCU for the
restart interval (T.81 A.2.2). That is exactly the 0x11 geometry, so this
re-derives the traversal as 1:1 and skips the chroma reads. It is not an
approximation: it is the traversal the standard prescribes for this scan,
which is why the subsampled case needs no separate handling.

Only luma is read here (JPEGPutMCU8BitGray uses the Y MCU alone, and the
chroma path is MCU_SKIP for grayscale output), so color output is refused
with JPEG_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE rather than composed against chroma blocks that
were never decoded. A single-component first scan that is not the luma DC
scan is refused the same way.

Verified with a host harness on a six-file corpus: baseline, interleaved
progressive at 4:4:4 and 4:2:0, grayscale progressive, and both
non-interleaved variants. The four unaffected files decode byte-identically
to before the patch; the two broken ones now produce bytes identical to the
interleaved encoding of the same source image.
---
 src/jpeg.inl | 37 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/jpeg.inl b/src/jpeg.inl
index 1bd38b2..0000000 100644
--- a/src/jpeg.inl
+++ b/src/jpeg.inl
@@ -5014,7 +5014,40 @@
     cDCTable2 = pJPEG->JPCI[2].dc_tbl_no;
     cACTable2 = pJPEG->JPCI[2].ac_tbl_no;
     iDCPred0 = iDCPred1 = iDCPred2 = mcuCX = mcuCY = 0;
-    
+
+    // CrossPoint patch: a progressive JPEG may carry its DC coefficients in one scan PER
+    // COMPONENT (SOS with a single component, Ss=0 Se=0) rather than in one interleaved scan.
+    // MozJPEG and most web optimizers emit that layout, so it is common in ripped and scanned
+    // material -- see crosspoint T-015.
+    //
+    // The MCU loop below reads a Y block (four, when subsampled) and then the chroma blocks of
+    // the same MCU. In a non-interleaved scan those chroma bits are not there, so the reads
+    // consume bits belonging to the FOLLOWING Y blocks and the bitstream desynchronizes. On a
+    // large image that surfaces as a Huffman failure (a bare JPEG_DECODE_ERROR); on a small one
+    // it does not fail at all and the preview is silently scrambled, which is the worse half.
+    //
+    // In a non-interleaved scan the data units are in raster order over the COMPONENT's own
+    // block grid -- ceil(width/8) x ceil(height/8) for luma, whatever the subsampling -- and one
+    // data unit counts as one MCU for the restart interval (T.81 A.2.2). That is exactly the
+    // 0x11 geometry, so re-deriving the traversal as 1:1 is not an approximation; it is the
+    // traversal the standard prescribes for this scan, which is why the subsampled case needs no
+    // separate handling. Only luma is read here, so color output is refused rather than
+    // composed against chroma blocks that were never decoded.
+    const int bSingleCompScan = (pJPEG->ucMode == 0xc2 && pJPEG->ucComponentsInScan == 1 &&
+                                 pJPEG->ucNumComponents > 1);
+    if (bSingleCompScan &&
+        (pJPEG->ucPixelType < EIGHT_BIT_GRAYSCALE || !pJPEG->JPCI[0].component_needed ||
+         pJPEG->iScanStart != 0 || pJPEG->iScanEnd != 0))
+    {
+        // Colour output, or a first scan that is not the luma DC scan: nothing here can produce
+        // the right pixels, so say so instead of rendering noise.
+        pJPEG->iError = JPEG_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE;
+        return 0;
+    }
+    const int bNonInterleavedDC = bSingleCompScan;
+    if (bNonInterleavedDC)
+        pJPEG->ucSubSample = 0x11; // for this scan the luma block grid IS the MCU grid
+
     switch (pJPEG->ucSubSample) // set up the parameters for the different subsampling options
     {
         case 0x00: // fake value to handle grayscale
@@ -5227,7 +5260,9 @@
                     }
                 } // if 2:2 subsampling
             } // if subsampling used
-            if (pJPEG->ucSubSample && pJPEG->ucNumComponents == 3) // if color (not CMYK)
+            // CrossPoint patch: a non-interleaved DC scan carries no chroma bits at all, so these
+            // reads must not happen -- they would eat the next Y block. See bNonInterleavedDC above.
+            if (pJPEG->ucSubSample && pJPEG->ucNumComponents == 3 && !bNonInterleavedDC) // if color (not CMYK)
             {
                 // first chroma
                 pJPEG->ucACTable = cACTable1;
